Looking at this problem, I need to prove that `K.Total(s + t) == K.Total(s) + K.Total(t)` by induction on `|s|`.

Let me analyze the available tools:
- `End.Base(s, t)` - requires `|s| == 0`, proves the goal
- `End.Direct(s, t)` - requires `|s| > 0` and `K.Total(s[1..] + t) == K.Total(s[1..]) + K.Total(t)`, proves the goal
- `Prove(s, t)` - the recursive call with decreasing `|s|`

The strategy is:
1. Base case: when `|s| == 0`, call `End.Base(s, t)`
2. Inductive case: when `|s| > 0`, first recursively call `Prove(s[1..], t)` to establish `K.Total(s[1..] + t) == K.Total(s[1..]) + K.Total(t)`, then call `End.Direct(s, t)`

Let me verify the decreases clause: `Prove(s, t)` has `decreases |s|`. When we call `Prove(s[1..], t)`, we have `|s[1..]| == |s| - 1 < |s|` when `|s| > 0`, so this terminates.

